.TH TOUCH 1
.SH NAME
touch \- update the date of a file
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B touch
[
.B \-c
]
file ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Touch
sets the accessed and modified dates of each
.I file
to the current time.
.PP
If a
.I file
does not exist,
an attempt will be made to create it unless the
.B \-c
option is specified.
.PP
.B Note:
this DESCRIPTION is not v7's, because this program is not quite v7's.
v7's
.I touch
moved a date by
.B "reading a character from the file and writing it back"
\- it did not call
.IR utime (2)
at all \- and its page said so.  This port calls
.IR utime (2),
which is a deliberate divergence and the only one in its task.  What v7's
method cost:
.IP \(bu 3
it needed read
.B and
write permission, so a file its own owner could not open for writing could not
be dated at all;
.IP \(bu 3
it re-created, and so truncated, any file of zero length, there being no byte
in one to rewrite;
.IP \(bu 3
it put a write down a path where nothing was meant to change, so a full
filesystem could make it fail halfway.
.PP
None of that is true here.  In exchange,
.I touch
now sets
.B both
dates rather than the modified one alone, and it is gated by
.IR utime (2)'s
own rule \- the caller must be the file's owner or the super-user \- rather
than by the file's write permission.  See
.IR utime (2),
whose second argument on this system is a two-element
.B time_t
vector,
.I accessed
then
.IR updated ,
and not
.BR "struct utimbuf" .
.PP
.B Note:
.B \-c
is positional.  The argument list is scanned once, so a
.B \-c
written after a file name affects only the names that follow it, and
.B "touch a \-c b"
creates
.I a
and refuses
.IR b .
The SYNOPSIS above implies otherwise; the program is v7's in this and was left
alone.
.PP
.B Note:
a file
.I touch
creates has mode
.BR 0666 ,
less the file creation mask \- and the initial mask
.RB ( CMASK
in <sys/param.h>) is
.B 0
on this system, with no
.B umask
builtin in any shell here.  So it is world-writable.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.I Touch
exits with the number of files it could not date.  v7's exited with whatever
happened to be in the accumulator; both messages go to the standard error, and
their inconsistent forms are v7's and were kept.
.PP
.BI "touch: file " name " does not exist."
\-
.B \-c
was given.
.PP
.BI "Cannot touch " name
\- the file could not be created, or
.IR utime (2)
refused it.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
chmod(1),
ls(1),
utime(2)
